1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,336
AI can help me ship a fix quickly.

2
00:00:03,336 --> 00:00:09,592
But when someone asks why I chose that approach, I sometimes struggle to explain it.

3
00:00:09,592 --> 00:00:16,682
And at the end of the day, I can forget what I changed and what I learned.

4
00:00:16,682 --> 00:00:22,520
Own the Fix helps developers ship the code while keeping ownership of the decisions.

5
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:28,359
This video uses an illustrated conversation and sample answers, not a live Discord recording.

6
00:00:28,359 --> 00:00:30,444
Here is our reproducible example.

7
00:00:30,444 --> 00:00:33,364
I search for C A, then cats.

8
00:00:33,364 --> 00:00:35,449
The cats request finishes first.

9
00:00:35,449 --> 00:00:39,619
But the older request arrives later and replaces those results.

10
00:00:39,619 --> 00:00:42,539
The latest search should own the screen.

11
00:00:42,539 --> 00:00:47,960
Instead of immediately giving me a patch, the workflow asks for my hypothesis.

12
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:52,131
The Switch-connected agent explains the actual source in plain language.

13
00:00:52,131 --> 00:00:56,718
Then I predict what will happen before seeing the test evidence.

14
00:00:56,718 --> 00:01:05,059
Next, I compare two approaches: ignore outdated results, or cancel older requests and still check whether their results are current.

15
00:01:05,059 --> 00:01:09,229
I choose an approach and explain the tradeoff I accept.

16
00:01:09,229 --> 00:01:13,817
Only then can the tool apply the selected prepared demo patch.

17
00:01:13,817 --> 00:01:16,736
The original code has three failing tests.

18
00:01:16,736 --> 00:01:19,238
The selected fix passes all four.

19
00:01:19,238 --> 00:01:22,158
These are executed tests, not invented numbers.

20
00:01:22,158 --> 00:01:29,665
Before finishing, I explain the change in my own words and name something those tests do not prove.

21
00:01:29,665 --> 00:01:33,418
The decision receipt preserves my explanation, choice, and evidence.

22
00:01:33,418 --> 00:01:39,257
The daily recap also checks my GitHub PR activity while the app is running.

23
00:01:39,257 --> 00:01:43,010
It keeps PR metadata separate from my recorded learning.

24
00:01:43,010 --> 00:01:47,598
It does not guess what I learned from a PR title.

25
00:01:47,598 --> 00:01:50,934
Switch carries the real developer and agent conversation.

26
00:01:50,934 --> 00:01:55,104
Wispr Flow can turn my spoken answers into reviewed text.

27
00:01:55,104 --> 00:02:01,360
Own the Fix keeps the reasoning with the work: ship the fix, keep the understanding.
